WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.366
Module 2: people, roles and access.

00:00:02.366 --> 00:00:06.151
A Culture Dig runs inside the
client's own organization,

00:00:06.151 --> 00:00:10.951
and the people whose stories you
collect often have accounts there too.

00:00:10.951 --> 00:00:12.573
Some of them are admins.

00:00:12.573 --> 00:00:18.049
In about thirty-five minutes, you'll see
how Culture Dig decides what each person

00:00:18.049 --> 00:00:23.457
can see, and how to set up a program so
the raw evidence reaches only the people

00:00:23.457 --> 00:00:24.268
who need it.

00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:29.958
The product's own advice is the principle
for this module: pick the least access

00:00:29.958 --> 00:00:32.229
that lets each person do their job,

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:35.992
especially for the people whose
stories you're collecting.

00:00:35.992 --> 00:00:39.821
It matters more in a dig than
in a survey, for two reasons.

00:00:39.821 --> 00:00:44.752
Fieldwork is raw: observations record
what people said, by role and context,

00:00:44.752 --> 00:00:48.646
and in a small unit that can be
enough to recognize someone.

00:00:48.646 --> 00:00:53.382
And people spoke on a promise that
their words would never be attributed.

00:00:53.382 --> 00:00:57.211
Who can read the workspace is
part of keeping that promise.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:02.632
Here's the rule Culture Dig follows
every time someone opens a program.

00:01:02.632 --> 00:01:05.758
First it asks: are you
Mosaic platform staff?

00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:11.316
If so, you act as Program Lead, and reaching
into a client's program is audited.

00:01:11.316 --> 00:01:16.596
Next: are you an owner or admin of the
organization this program belongs to?

00:01:16.596 --> 00:01:21.389
If so, you also act as Program Lead,
whatever role you've been given.

00:01:21.389 --> 00:01:24.376
There's one exception,
which we'll come to.

00:01:24.376 --> 00:01:29.309
Only after that does it look at the
role you were given in the program,

00:01:29.309 --> 00:01:34.797
and only if you're still in the organization
and have accepted your invitation.

00:01:34.797 --> 00:01:38.270
Otherwise, the program
simply isn't there for you.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.119
The surprise for most people is step two.

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:45.426
An org admin you put on a research
team still sees everything.

00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:51.486
There's one way to scope an admin down:
give them an explicit Participant row in

00:01:51.486 --> 00:01:52.319
the program.

00:01:52.319 --> 00:01:54.678
The product's reasoning is simple.

00:01:54.678 --> 00:01:59.675
The people whose stories you're
collecting are often admins of their own

00:01:59.675 --> 00:02:03.562
organization, and a lead who
chose Participant meant it.

00:02:03.562 --> 00:02:04.673
Know its limits.

00:02:04.673 --> 00:02:09.600
It works on admins only; owners and
platform staff are always elevated,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.724
so an organization can
never lock itself out.

00:02:12.724 --> 00:02:17.374
It's set program by program, so a
second program needs its own row.

00:02:17.374 --> 00:02:20.358
And it's the only role
that removes access.

00:02:20.358 --> 00:02:25.563
A Team Member or Facilitator who is an
org admin is still a Program Lead in

00:02:25.563 --> 00:02:26.188
practice.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:29.921
Here's Northwind's
organization before the dig.

00:02:29.921 --> 00:02:34.580
Alex is the owner, Jamie Ortiz an
editor, and Jordan Reyes, the COO,

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:39.103
was made an admin in October to
read the Culture Progress Monitor.

00:02:39.103 --> 00:02:42.118
The dig is asking why
decisions feel closed,

00:02:42.118 --> 00:02:45.818
and many of those decisions
come from Jordan's office.

00:02:45.818 --> 00:02:50.341
If nothing changes, what will Jordan
see, and what should Alex do?

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:54.540
When a lead adds someone by
email, three things happen.

00:02:54.540 --> 00:02:57.573
If the person has no
account, one is created,

00:02:57.573 --> 00:03:00.875
with a password-setup link
that lasts seven days.

00:03:00.875 --> 00:03:03.773
If they aren't already
in the organization,

00:03:03.773 --> 00:03:08.423
they get a dig-only membership: it
reaches this dig and nothing else,

00:03:08.423 --> 00:03:11.861
so no other surveys,
responses or question library.

00:03:11.861 --> 00:03:16.309
Someone who already works there
keeps exactly the access they had.

00:03:16.309 --> 00:03:21.633
And an email explains the dig and their
role, and lands them on their own page.

00:03:21.633 --> 00:03:24.531
If the email fails,
they're still enrolled,

00:03:24.531 --> 00:03:29.181
and the roster shows the invitation
as not sent so you can resend it.

00:03:29.181 --> 00:03:32.888
One more rule: each person
holds one role in a program,

00:03:32.888 --> 00:03:35.921
and a researcher belongs
to exactly one team.

00:03:36.433 --> 00:03:38.677
There are three kinds of invitee.

00:03:38.677 --> 00:03:42.484
Someone with no account sets
a password, and they're in.

00:03:42.484 --> 00:03:47.855
Someone already on the client's staff
needs to do nothing; their role is simply

00:03:47.855 --> 00:03:48.263
added.

00:03:48.263 --> 00:03:53.091
And someone who already has an account
but isn't on the client's staff,

00:03:53.091 --> 00:03:57.306
including someone whose only link
is another dig, must accept.

00:03:57.306 --> 00:04:01.929
They sign in as that account and
press Accept, within fourteen days.

00:04:01.929 --> 00:04:05.057
Until then, the program
opens nothing to them.

00:04:05.057 --> 00:04:05.329
Why?

00:04:05.329 --> 00:04:09.952
Because anyone can register an
account under someone else's address.

00:04:09.952 --> 00:04:12.739
The accept link goes to the real mailbox.

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:16.819
Resending sends a fresh link,
and the old one stops working.

00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:18.267
Now practise.

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:22.574
It's March the twenty-third,
and Jamie shows you the roster.

00:04:22.574 --> 00:04:25.877
The roster only flags
people who need chasing,

00:04:25.877 --> 00:04:28.964
with a short line of
text under their name.

00:04:28.964 --> 00:04:31.189
No line means nothing to chase.

00:04:31.189 --> 00:04:34.348
Five people are worth
a look, including you.

00:04:34.348 --> 00:04:39.015
Work out what's going on with each
one, and what Jamie should do.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.647
Roster mistakes are easy to fix.

00:04:41.647 --> 00:04:44.330
Role and team are dropdowns on each row,

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.416
so you don't need to
remove and re-add anyone.

00:04:47.416 --> 00:04:52.916
Two guards stop you locking yourself out:
you can't remove or demote your own lead

00:04:52.916 --> 00:04:54.392
row, or the last lead.

00:04:54.392 --> 00:04:59.826
Removing someone takes their dig-only
membership with them, but never a real one.

00:04:59.826 --> 00:05:05.259
And remember why the product recommends
a second lead: the program isn't stuck if

00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:10.089
you're away, and a second lead who's
an org admin can buy the next wave.

00:05:10.600 --> 00:05:12.484
Let's check who can see what.

00:05:12.484 --> 00:05:15.798
Drag each item to every
role that can see or do it.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:18.562
Here's one that surprises people.

00:05:18.562 --> 00:05:22.881
A researcher can download the
stories told in their own domain.

00:05:22.881 --> 00:05:28.365
That's deliberate, because researchers
often want to work with their evidence in

00:05:28.365 --> 00:05:29.462
their own tools.

00:05:29.462 --> 00:05:33.643
It also means your researchers,
the storytellers' colleagues,

00:05:33.643 --> 00:05:35.220
can read those stories.

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:40.224
The file itself warns that unattributed
isn't the same as unidentifiable,

00:05:40.224 --> 00:05:45.776
and their invitation and the Story patterns
page both tell them to keep it within

00:05:45.776 --> 00:05:46.942
the program team.

00:05:46.942 --> 00:05:51.603
Still, brief researchers at kickoff
that story text is confidential,

00:05:51.603 --> 00:05:56.950
and think twice before putting someone
on a team whose domain covers their own

00:05:56.950 --> 00:05:57.704
small unit.

00:05:58.200 --> 00:06:01.212
The product controls
what each role can see.

00:06:01.212 --> 00:06:04.088
Who you put in each role is your judgment.

00:06:04.088 --> 00:06:07.032
Mix sites, levels and
tenure on every team.

00:06:07.032 --> 00:06:10.660
Don't have people interview
their own direct reports,

00:06:10.660 --> 00:06:13.947
because people don't speak
freely to their boss.

00:06:13.947 --> 00:06:19.013
Don't put someone on a team whose own
decisions are that domain's subject.

00:06:19.013 --> 00:06:22.162
And brief every researcher
on non-attribution.

00:06:22.162 --> 00:06:26.270
Choose leads who can hold raw
evidence without acting on it.

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:31.747
A lead who reads an interview and then
has a word with the manager described has

00:06:31.747 --> 00:06:34.486
broken the promise to every interviewee.

00:06:34.486 --> 00:06:37.430
Executives receive
findings, not fieldwork.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.812
And tell every interviewee what
the dig is, that it's voluntary,

00:06:41.812 --> 00:06:44.071
and how their words will be used.

00:06:44.567 --> 00:06:48.563
Time for the module check: six
questions, and you need five right.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:53.292
Here's your Access and Invitation
Checklist: owners and admins,

00:06:53.292 --> 00:06:58.658
who gets which role, what the roster
statuses mean, and the researcher briefing.

00:06:58.658 --> 00:06:59.463
Download it.

00:06:59.967 --> 00:07:00.671
To sum up.

00:07:00.671 --> 00:07:04.826
Org owners and admins act as
Program Lead in every program.

00:07:04.826 --> 00:07:10.179
A Participant row is the one way to
scope an admin down, program by program.

00:07:10.179 --> 00:07:15.532
New accounts get a seven-day setup link,
the client's staff need do nothing,

00:07:15.532 --> 00:07:19.757
and existing accounts elsewhere
accept within fourteen days.

00:07:19.757 --> 00:07:23.490
Dig-only membership reaches
the dig and nothing else.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:28.209
And researchers can read their own
domain's stories, so brief them,

00:07:28.209 --> 00:07:30.040
and staff teams with care.

00:07:30.040 --> 00:07:32.646
Next: planning and running fieldwork.

